  "notes": [
    "model.fit width 1.035: clean measured the body 0.922x the top drawing's width, but the front/rear silhouettes were already 0.98x (the top drawing's tyres and rear arches bulge wider than the front/rear drawings). 1.045 left thin cyan bands on both sides of the front/rear silhouettes (1.02x), so 1.035 is the balance: top ~0.955, front/rear ~1.01 - inside 5% everywhere without making the car wide",
    "model.glass.remove: the sheet's window finder took dark non-glass for glass - the black wing endplates (side, left, top, rear), the rear tyres in the top view and, worst, the black bonnet/roof stripe between the top view's windows (x 293-520). Removed so those drawings paint them as bodywork (the stripe on the roof comes from the top drawing)",
    "model.rebake.glass: all five views traced on the Tripo mesh from its own dark window paint (ripcars outline + per-column profiles of qa tripo-sheet.png), checked on the clay. Side/left: Tripo left the door windows as open holes (nothing to paint), so only the rear quarter window (inset ~8 px from the hole edge to keep a thin B-pillar, ~3 px from the black frame) and the windscreen's edge-on strip over the A-pillar. Top (unseen, mirrored about y 953): windscreen from its roof edge to the curved front edge, rear window on the mesh's panel x 185-330 (the drawing's sits ~30 px further back; Tripo's striped paint near the roof is covered), quarter-glass strip; the door-hole strip and wing stay out. Front/rear (mirrored): windscreen down to the cowl; rear window inside its recess below the wing, without the dark C-pillar slivers beside it. No tinted glass: the toy's windows are clear smoke with the cabin visible",
    "model.glass.remove side/left quarter windows: through the mesh's quarter glass the side drawings 'saw' the rear-window recess wall and painted it with the drawing's light-grey glass (a white patch beside the rear window from above/behind); without the mask there those views don't paint what's behind the quarter glass. top x 148-184: the drawing's rear window sits ~25 px behind the mesh's, so on the mesh that strip is the lip below the glass - unmasked, the top drawing paints it instead of Tripo's invented louvre stripes",
    "model.rebake.glass side/left rear-window strips: the rear window's side edge folds over the roofline, and the side drawings painted it with their light grey silhouette edge (a white wedge on the rear window seen from behind). A thin strip along the mesh's dark roofline edge (x 180-340 side, 686-842 left) makes those texels glass too"
  ]
}
